      <section id="tasks" class="doc glass-heavy shine reveal" aria-labelledby="tasks-h">
        <div class="prose">
          <p class="eyebrow">09</p>
          <h2 id="tasks-h" class="h2-sm">Tasks</h2>
          <p>
            Tasks are native, with no external tracker to mirror. The model is spaces, then initiatives, then
            tasks, then comments, with sub-tasks, start dates and soft dependencies. One shared write surface
            in <code>hq-db</code> backs the MCP tools (<code>task_list</code>, <code>task_create</code>,
            <code>task_update</code>, <code>task_comment_add</code> and friends), the REST API with its
            WebSocket broadcast, and the web UI with list, board and timeline views. Tasks have no CLI command.
          </p>
          <p>
            Tagging a task routes it to an agent mailbox synchronously. A person's tasks live in a private space
            that only that person's turns can read. See <a href={links.nativeTasks}>the design note</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="doc-viz">
          <div class="viz kanban" role="img" aria-label="A linked task moves from in progress to ready for review. Moving it to complete is left to you.">
            <span class="kcol"><span class="label">In progress</span><span class="kcard"></span></span>
            <span class="kcol"><span class="label">Ready for review</span></span>
            <span class="kcol kcol-done"><span class="label">Complete</span><span class="small">Only you move it here</span></span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="governance" class="doc flip glass-heavy shine reveal" aria-labelledby="governance-h">
        <div class="prose">
          <p class="eyebrow">10</p>
          <h2 id="governance-h" class="h2-sm">Governance and the bash sandbox</h2>
          <p>
            The agent's <code>bash</code> tool runs model-written shell commands, so HQ does not rely on the
            model refusing. A governance layer denies dangerous effects mechanically, before a tool runs, and it
            applies in permissive modes too.
          </p>
          <p>
            Credential files such as <code>~/.ssh</code>, cloud credentials and the HQ config are unreachable
            always. A session becomes tainted once a tool returns untrusted content: web fetch and search, mail,
            converted documents, other agents' mailboxes, session logs, pane reads, vault reads and any remote
            MCP result. Taint is shared with every sub-agent the session spawns, so delegating cannot launder
            it. After taint, project secrets such as <code>.env</code> files and outbound network from bash
            (<code>curl</code>, <code>ssh</code>, <code>nc</code> and similar) are denied.
          </p>
          <p>
            For bash, three layers follow. Text checks catch honest mistakes and cheap tricks, but about 100
            known obfuscation shapes are pinned in tests and some still get through, so they are speed bumps.
            An environment allowlist starts the child with an empty environment and a short list of variables.
            HQ's own keys are refused even if you list them. Then the OS sandbox: bubblewrap on Linux,
            <code>sandbox-exec</code> on macOS. On Linux it gives a read-only root, a fresh <code>/proc</code>
            in a new pid namespace, writable binds only for home, the working directory, temp and the vault, and
            optionally no network. Config, env files and private SSH keys are masked.
          </p>
          <p>
            <code>governance.bash.sandbox</code> takes <code>off</code>, <code>best_effort</code> or
            <code>required</code>. After 0.9.0 the default is <code>required</code>: if no sandbox backend
            works, every bash command is refused and <code>hq doctor</code> says so.
          </p>
          <p class="small">What this does not cover is on the <a href="/security/">security page</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="doc-viz">
          <div class="viz gov" role="img" aria-label="Commands pass through text checks, the environment allowlist with taint, and the OS sandbox. A plain command passes; a curl after untrusted content is denied.">
            <span class="packet pk1">ls notes/</span>
            <span class="packet pk2">curl (tainted)</span>
            <span class="glayer glass-light gl1"><span>Text checks</span><span class="label">Speed bumps</span></span>
            <span class="glayer glass-light"><span>Env allowlist and taint</span><span class="label l-deny">Denies</span></span>
            <span class="glayer gl-os"><span>OS sandbox</span><span class="label l-req">Required</span></span>
          </div>
          <details class="more"><summary>Full diagram and text description</summary><Diagram d={governance} /></details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="updates" class="doc glass-heavy shine reveal" aria-labelledby="updates-h">
        <div class="prose">
          <p class="eyebrow">11</p>
          <h2 id="updates-h" class="h2-sm">The signed release and pull-updater pipeline</h2>
          <p>
            A server instance pulls its updates. Nothing on the build side holds a credential for any instance,
            and instances hold only a public key.
          </p>
          <h3>Build side</h3>
          <p>
            Every merge to <code>main</code> runs tests, builds on an older Ubuntu image so the binary needs
            only an older glibc, and publishes a signed prerelease named like <code>0.9.1-main.6</code>. The job
            that runs repository build code never sees the signing key: a separate sign job does. Each release
            carries a binary archive per platform (Linux x86_64, Linux ARM and Apple Silicon), the web bundle,
            <code>SHA256SUMS</code>, a <code>manifest.json</code> and its <code>.minisig</code>. Channel pointers
            (<code>channel-main</code>, <code>channel-stable</code>) are small signed JSON files that say which
            release a channel currently means. Promoting to stable signs a new pointer for an existing release.
            Nothing is rebuilt.
          </p>
          <h3>Instance side</h3>
          <ol class="facts">
            <li>A systemd timer fires every 10 minutes with a short random delay and runs <code>hq update --apply</code> under an exclusive lock.</li>
            <li>It verifies the pointer signature, checks that the manifest URL lives under the configured repository, then verifies the manifest signature and its SHA-256, the schema and the minimum updater version.</li>
            <li>Equal or lower versions do nothing. A pointer with a lower sequence number than one already accepted is refused as a replay.</li>
            <li>It downloads with hard size caps, verifies each artifact's SHA-256 against the signed manifest, and unpacks with a strict tar reader that rejects links, devices, absolute and parent paths and duplicates.</li>
            <li>It snapshots the vault database with <code>VACUUM INTO</code>, as the service user, never as root.</li>
            <li>It runs the staged binary as the service user with <code>--version</code> and requires the exact build. This is the only downloaded code that runs before the swap.</li>
            <li>It swaps the binary and web files by rename, keeping the last three binaries and one old web tree, restarts the service and polls <code>/health</code> until it reports the new git sha.</li>
            <li>On success it runs <code>hq install --upgrade</code>. On failure it rolls back, restores the database only if migrations ran, blocks that version for 24 hours and sends an alert. A crash mid-update is recovered from a journal on the next run.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Signatures are minisign (Ed25519), verified in process. The key is read from a root-owned file or
            compiled in, and cannot be overridden by an environment variable. Key rotation is a manual step. A
            compromised signing key and a stale-pointer freeze attack are listed as not mitigated. Read
            <a href={links.updateSystem}>docs/UPDATE_SYSTEM.md</a> for the contract.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="doc-viz">
          <ol class="viz usteps" aria-label="The eight steps an instance runs on every update, lighting in turn.">
            {updateSteps.map((s, i) => <li class={`us${i}`}><b>{i + 1}</b>{s}</li>)}
          </ol>
          <details class="more"><summary>Full diagram and text description</summary><Diagram d={releasePipeline} /></details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="topologies" class="doc flip glass-heavy shine reveal" aria-labelledby="topologies-h">
        <div class="prose">
          <p class="eyebrow">12</p>
          <h2 id="topologies-h" class="h2-sm">Deployment topologies</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Laptop only.</strong> Run <code>hq start all</code>. The web server binds to loopback. Set
            <code>web_auth_token</code> on a shared machine. This is the default and needs no network setup.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>VPS plus Tailscale.</strong> Provision a Debian or Ubuntu box with
            <code>deploy/setup-vps.sh</code>, install the signed updater with <code>deploy/install.sh</code>,
            and keep HQ on loopback. Caddy bridges to <code>tailscale serve</code>, which terminates HTTPS on
            your tailnet only, so the UI never touches the public internet. List every origin you serve the UI
            from in <code>web_allowed_origins</code>. HQ on the VPS can then drive the host on a laptop over the
            gated ssh key.
          </p>
          <p>
            The only endpoint that may ever be public is <code>/mcp</code>, through a proxy that exposes
            <code>/mcp</code> and <code>/health</code> and nothing else, with an API key set. <code>/ws</code>
            and <code>/api</code> must never be public. A host runs one deploy path only: the pull updater, or a
            manual build, not both. Steps are on the <a href="/install/">install page</a> and in
            <a href={links.deployReadme}>deploy/README.md</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="doc-viz">
          <div class="viz topo-viz" role="img" aria-label="A request travels from a phone over the tailnet to Caddy on loopback and then to HQ. Only /mcp and /health may be public.">
            <span class="label">VPS plus Tailscale</span>
            <span class="tp-track"><span class="tpk"></span></span>
            <span class="topo">
              <span class="pill glass-light">Phone</span><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8250;</span>
              <span class="pill glass-light">Tailnet HTTPS</span><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8250;</span>
              <span class="pill glass-light">Caddy 127.0.0.1</span><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8250;</span>
              <span class="pill pill-green">HQ on loopback</span>
            </span>
            <span class="public glass-light"><strong>Public:</strong> only <code>/mcp</code> and <code>/health</code>, with a key.</span>
          </div>
          <details class="more"><summary>Full diagram and text description</summary><Diagram d={topologies} /></details>
        </div>
      </section>

